(1047,'Parameter reference epoch','The reference epoch for the parameters of a time-dependent transformation.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-10-16','2013.021 2016.061',false),
(1048,'Interpolation CRS code','The EPSG code for the CRS that should be used to interpolate gridded data.','OGP','IOGP','2016-11-08','2016.031',false),
(1049,'Transformation reference epoch','The epoch at which a time-specific transformation is valid.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.061',false),
(1050,'Point motion velocity grid file','The name of the [path and] grid file containing velocities.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1051,'Unit conversion scalar','Ratio of [(factor b) / (factor c)] from the EPSG Dataset Unit of Measure table, populated with respect to the SI base unit, e.g. ft/m.','IOGP','IOGP','2018-01-19','2014.027 2018.004',false),
(1052,'Point motion velocity X','The velocity of a point resolved into the geocentric Cartesian X axis direction.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1053,'Point motion velocity Y','The velocity of a point resolved into the geocentric Cartesian Y axis direction.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1054,'Point motion velocity Z','The velocity of a point resolved into the geocentric Cartesian Z axis direction.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1055,'Point motion velocity north','The component in the north direction of a point''s velocity.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1056,'Point motion velocity east','The component in the east direction of a point''s velocity.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1057,'Point motion velocity up','The component in the up direction of a point''s velocity.','IOGP','IOGP','2017-09-23','2016.060',false),
(1058,'Ellipsoidal height difference file','The name of the [path and] file containing ellipsoidal height differences.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2018-04-10','2018.017',false),
(1059,'Wellbore point scale factor (psf) flag','Indicator for point scale factor application in wellbore local to projected CRS coordinate transformations: 0 =  no point scale factor; 1 = constant point scale factor calculated at first point in wellbore and applied throughout; 2 = variable point scale factor calculated throughout the wellbore path.','IOGP','IOGP','2019-02-11','2017.040',false),
(1060,'Wellbore depth correction factor (dcf) flag','Indicator for application of depth correction factor applied in wellbore local to geographic and projected CRS coordinate transformations: 0 for no depth correction; 2 for a variable depth correction factor calculated throughout  the wellbore path.','IOGP','IOGP','2019-02-11','2017.040',false),
(1061,'Scale factor for source CRS axes','Multiplication factor applied to coordinates in the source coordinate reference system to obtain the correct scale in the target coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-19','2019.004',false),
(8601,'Latitude offset','The difference between the latitude values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8602,'Longitude offset','The difference between the longitude values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8603,'Vertical Offset','The difference between the height or depth values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8604,'Geoid undulation','The height offset. "Geoid undulation" is used loosely in the parameter name. If the offset is between the ellipsoid and the geoid then the offset will be the geoid separation N. When the offset is between the ellipsoid and a realisation of the geoid through a vertical datum then the offsets C will form a height correction surface. A height correction surface differs from a geoid model by small amounts due to the difference between the geoid and the actual datum surface as well as some other assumptions regarding the gravity field.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2008-04-11','2008.010',false),
(8605,'X-axis translation','The difference between the X values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8606,'Y-axis translation','The difference between the Y values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8607,'Z-axis translation','The difference between the Z values of a point in the target and source coordinate reference systems.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8608,'X-axis rotation','The angular difference between the Y and Z axes directions of target and source coordinate reference systems. This is a rotation about the X axis as viewed from the origin looking along that axis. The particular method defines which direction is positive, and what is being rotated (point or axis).','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8609,'Y-axis rotation','The angular difference between the X and Z axes directions of target and source coordinate reference systems. This is a rotation about theY axis as viewed from the origin looking along that axis. The particular method defines which direction is positive, and what is being rotated (point or axis).','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8610,'Z-axis rotation','The angular difference between the X and Y axes directions of target and source coordinate reference systems. This is a rotation about the Z axis as viewed from the origin looking along that axis. The particular method defines which direction is positive, and what is being rotated (point or axis).','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','OGP','2013-07-21','2013.021',false),
(8611,'Scale difference','Scale factor for source CRS axes minus one, where the scale factor is a multiplication factor to be applied to coordinates in the source CRS to obtain the correct scale in the target CRS. dS = (M – 1). If a distance of 100 km in the source CRS translates into a distance of 100.001 km in the target CRS, the scale difference is 1 ppm (the ratio being 1.000001). When the scale difference dS is expressed in parts per million (ppm), M = (1 + dS*10-6). When the scale difference dS is expressed in parts per billion (ppb), M = (1 + dS*10-9).','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2013.021 2019.006',false),
(8612,'Scale factor for source CRS first axis','Multiplication factor applied to coordinates in first axis of the the source coordinate reference system to obtain the correct scale in the corresponding axis in the target coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2019.004',false),
(8613,'Scale factor for source CRS second axis','Multiplication factor applied to coordinates in the second axis of the source coordinate reference system to obtain the correct scale in the corresponding axis in the target coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2019.004',false),
(8614,'Rotation angle of source CRS axes','Angle (counter-clockwise positive) through which both of the source coordinate reference system axes need to be rotated to coincide with the corresponding target coordinate reference system axes.  ¶¶Alternatively, the bearing (clockwise positive) of the source coordinate reference system Y-axis measured relative to target coordinate reference system north.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2019.004',false),
(8615,'Rotation angle of source CRS first axis','Angle (counter-clockwise positive) through which the source coordinate reference system''s first axis needs to be rotated to coincide with the corresponding axis of the target coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2019.004',false),
(8616,'Rotation angle of source CRS second axis','Angle (counter-clockwise positive) through which the source coordinate reference system''s second axis needs to be rotated to coincide with the corresponding axis of the target coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7-2.','IOGP','2019-02-21','2019.004',false),
(8617,'Ordinate 1 of evaluation point','The value of the first ordinate value of the evaluation point.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8618,'Ordinate 2 of evaluation point','The value of the second ordinate of the evaluation point.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8619,'Ordinate 1 of evaluation point in source CRS','The value of the first ordinate of the evaluation point expressed in the source coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8620,'Ordinate 2 of evaluation point in source CRS','The value of the second ordinate of the evaluation point expressed in the source coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8621,'Ordinate 1 of evaluation point in target CRS','The value of the first ordinate of the evaluation point expressed in the target coordinate reference system.  In the case of an affine transformation the evaluation point is the origin of the source coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
(8622,'Ordinate 2 of evaluation point in target CRS','The value of the second ordinate of the evaluation point expressed in the target coordinate reference system.  In the case of an affine transformation the evaluation point is the origin of the source coordinate reference system.','EPSG guidance note number 7.','OGP','2000-06-23','',false),
