(1184,'European Terrestrial Reference Frame 1996','geodetic','Fixed to the stable part of the Eurasian continental plate at epoch 1989.0 and coincides with ITRF89 at epoch 1989.0. Defined by transformation from ITRF96 - see code 7938.','1989-01-01',7019,8901,1298,'Geodesy.','Replaces ETRF94 (code 1183). Replaced by ETRF97 (code 1185).','IAG Subcommision for the European Reference Frame (EUREF, http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/) and Boucher and Altamimi "Memo : Specifications for reference frame fixing in the analysis of a EUREF GPS campaign" (http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/memo-V8.pdf)','IOGP','2016-11-08','2015.013',false),
(1185,'European Terrestrial Reference Frame 1997','geodetic','Fixed to the stable part of the Eurasian continental plate at epoch 1989.0 and coincides with ITRF89 at epoch 1989.0. Defined by transformation from ITRF97 - see code 7939.','1989-01-01',7019,8901,1298,'Geodesy.','Replaces ETRF96 (code 1184). Replaced by ETRF2000 (code 1186).','IAG Subcommision for the European Reference Frame (EUREF, http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/) and Boucher and Altamimi "Memo : Specifications for reference frame fixing in the analysis of a EUREF GPS campaign" (http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/memo-V8.pdf)','IOGP','2016-11-08','2015.013',false),
(1186,'European Terrestrial Reference Frame 2000','geodetic','Fixed to the stable part of the Eurasian continental plate at epoch 1989.0 and coincides with ITRF89 at epoch 1989.0. Defined by transformation from ITRF2000 - see code 7940.','1989-01-01',7019,8901,1298,'Geodesy.','Replaces ETRF97 (code 1185). On the publication of ETRF2005 (code 1204),  the EUREF Technical Working Group recommended that ETRF2000 be the realization of ETRS89. ETRF2014 (code 1206) is technically superior to all earlier realizations of ETRS89.','IAG Subcommision for the European Reference Frame (EUREF, http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/) and Boucher and Altamimi "Memo : Specifications for reference frame fixing in the analysis of a EUREF GPS campaign" (http://etrs89.ensg.ign.fr/memo-V8.pdf)','IOGP','2018-02-16','2015.013 2018.008',false),
